# .unattended.conf.example — copy to the ADOPTING repo's root as `.unattended.conf` and fill it in.
#
# THIS FILE IS A TEMPLATE, NOT A DEFAULT. It shipped once as a byte copy of the authoring repo's own
# conf, which meant an adopter who copied it inherited that repo's lander, that repo's merge bar and
# — worse — that repo's MEASURED pins. A pin copied from another corpus is either vacuous or
# permanently red; this fleet tracks that as a named bug class. Every value below is either a
# placeholder to replace or a number to MEASURE against your own tree.
#
# Contract: <MEMORY_ROOT>/guides/UNATTENDED-PROTOCOL.md (binding). The driver and the gate leg READ
# these declarations; neither restates one. A phase token or a DoD item spelled into a script is a
# defect the leg reds on.

# The memory tree, matching your .memory-tree.conf.
MEMORY_ROOT=memory

# REPLACE. The mandated landing command. If your pre-push hook refuses an un-marked default-branch
# push, name the script that sets the marker — it exists so the remote is reconciled BEFORE the gate.
LANDER="bash tools/push-main.sh"

# REPLACE if your hook uses a different bypass. The close path must never emit this, and the leg
# greps the run-state record for it in both directions.
BYPASS_BAN="--no-verify"

# REPLACE. Your full merge bar, for the `gates-green` DoD item.
GATE_CMD="bash tools/run-gates/run-gates.sh"

# The wall-clock bound, in seconds, on any command this driver runs that the PROJECT declared --
# GATE_CMD and WIRING_CHECK. OPTIONAL: leave it out and the kit default applies, announced on
# stderr so a defaulted bound is never a silent one. A non-numeric or zero value is a REFUSAL,
# because 0 means no bound at all and a bound nobody can parse is a bound nobody set.
#
# Set it ABOVE your slowest legitimate bar. A bound that fires on a healthy run gets deleted.
GATE_BOUND="3600"

# REPLACE. The NON-repairing wiring check `--preflight` delegates to. Only read-only flags are
# accepted (--check, --dry-run, --verify, -n): a repairing mode sets git config and rewrites tracked
# bytes, and the run's first act must not be that.
WIRING_CHECK="bash tools/check-wiring.sh --check"

# REPLACE with YOUR harness's scheduler tool names. These are named for the AGENT to call — no
# script can reach the store, which is in-memory and session-scoped. The driver only records the id
# the agent hands it and later asserts a recorded reap.
KEEPALIVE_CREATE="<your-schedule-create-tool>"
KEEPALIVE_DELETE="<your-schedule-delete-tool>"

# The cadence the agent schedules the keepalive at. Rendered into the Skill as prose, so an
# UNDECLARED value now leaves its own placeholder standing and reds the adopter check rather than
# rendering "at the cadence this project declares — ." with every gate green.
KEEPALIVE_INTERVAL="<e.g. every 10 minutes (cron 3-59/10 * * * *)>"

# MEASURE, do not copy. `<phases>:<dod>` — the SIZE of the kit's core sets as your installed copy
# ships them. Count them in `unattended.sh` (`PHASES_CORE`, `DOD_CORE`) and write the two numbers
# here. Undeclared or malformed leaves BOTH shrink-only pins unenforced, so both are refusals.
#
# UPDATING FROM unattended 1.12 OR EARLIER: the DoD half moves 10 -> 11. Kit 1.13 adds the core item
# `reuse-probed`, and the floor is shrink-only, so leaving it at 10 makes `check-unattended.sh`
# check 3 red with "the declared floor sits below the kit's own core count". Bump it when you take
# the version.
CORE_FLOOR="12:12"

# The recall CLI whose query log the `reuse-probed` Definition-of-Done item reads. REPO-RELATIVE
# path, and BLANK is the default and means "not adopted": the item then reports an announced SKIP
# rather than an unmeetable UNMET, so a project that took this kit and not the recall kit is not
# wedged by a core item it can never satisfy.
#
# A DECLARATION rather than a path in the driver, for the reason every other key here exists: gov
# ships its own bytes verbatim, so a `tools/<kit>/` literal in a script arrives unchanged in a tree
# installed at another prefix and resolves to nothing there — `tools/check-install-prefix.sh` reds
# on exactly that. You know where your recall kit lives; the driver does not.
#
# It is NOT a `requires_if` edge in kit.toml. `govkit` resolves such an edge against a condition key,
# and conditioning "needs the recall kit" on "declares the recall kit" is a tautology, not a
# dependency.
RECALL_CLI=""

# OPTIONAL. The kickoff engine whose unattended hand-back the gate reads; BLANK turns that check off,
# which is correct for a repo with no kickoff skill. KICKOFF_EXITS is a shrink-only floor on how many
# interactive exits that engine resolves without an owner turn — MEASURE it against your own engine,
# because the count is a property of that document and not of this kit.
KICKOFF_ENGINE=""
KICKOFF_EXITS=""

# Project EXTENSIONS to the kit-owned core sets, space-separated. Empty is legal and means "core
# only"; it is NOT the same as an empty core set, which is a refusal.
# The DEFAULT DIRECTIVE SET is kit-owned and MUST-by-default; this is the extension point, and
# DIRECTIVES_FLOOR is the shrink-only pin on the core count. Separate from CORE_FLOOR on purpose:
# that key has a recorded failure where ONE malformed spelling disarms BOTH its pins while the
# conf still looks configured, and a third field would make one typo disarm three.
DIRECTIVES_EXTRA=""
# The project-owned ROW SOURCE for anything DIRECTIVES_EXTRA declares: a repo-relative markdown
# file carrying rows in the Skill table shape. Undeclared is the EMPTY SET, which is what every
# adopter has today. Without it an extra handle is waivable by a verb and invisible to the agent,
# and a project cannot fix that by editing the Skill, which is rendered from a kit template.
DIRECTIVES_EXTRA_TABLE=""
DIRECTIVES_FLOOR="16"

# Which anchors may authorize a run. CLOSED value set: `default-branch` (the build README must
# resolve at the merge-base with the tip the remote advertises for its own HEAD) or `published`
# (that anchor first, then the tip the remote advertises for the run's OWN branch, provided it is an
# ancestor of HEAD). ABSENT, BLANK or anything outside the set keeps `default-branch` — a
# misspelling may not silently select either behaviour.
#
# `published` SPENDS three things: a run can authorize itself rather than only its successor, since
# a branch push is not gated by the pre-push hook; the bar's BASE check widens for EVERY adopter,
# because this file is a working-tree file the run can commit and so the leg cannot read it; and
# roster integrity stops being enforceable on the branch anchor. Protocol section 1 carries them.
# TOOL-aPromptedMandate-5 - this key also gates the Skill's PROMPT start path. Left blank (the
# default, and what this example ships) a run can only be started from a build folder somebody
# already landed; the prompt path is rendered as unavailable and says so, rather than printing a
# procedure that ends in a refusal. Declaring `published` turns it on and accepts the three costs
# section 1 of the protocol enumerates.
#
# TOOL-dNarrowedAnchor-1 - and it spends them PER MODE, not per project. The second anchor is
# admissible only for a build README declaring a discipline that AUTHORS that folder: `prompt` and
# `recipe`, which the driver publishes as `SECOND_ANCHOR_MODES`. A `slug` README - and an absent
# `authorized-by:` key IS `slug` - is refused there, because `slug` names a folder that already
# exists and a `slug` run reaching the branch anchor has contradicted its own declaration. So turning
# this key on no longer widens every run in the project; it widens the two disciplines that say, in
# the file the bar reads, that they author their own authorization. The leg re-derives the same
# verdict from the recorded BASE and the advertised default-branch tip, so cost 2 above is no longer
# the whole story: the leg cannot read THIS file, but it can read that.
ANCHOR_SCOPE=""

# TOOL-aNamedGesture-1 - the TOKEN that authorizes a prompt-mode run. The Skill's prompt path is
# entered only when the invocation carries it, so this is the gesture that separates a description of
# a build from a run that will merge and push with no owner turn. Its ARGUMENT is the build: either a
# path to a file holding the prompt, or the prompt itself.
#
# BLANK is the kit default and is what every adopter should ship. The default literal lives in
# adopt-unattended.sh and nowhere else, and the RENDERED Skill states it verbatim - so read the value
# out of .claude/skills/unattended/SKILL.md rather than expecting this file to name it. Declare a
# value here only to override the spelling, which is worth doing in a project whose ordinary prose
# would contain the default.
#
# WHAT IS REFUSED at render time, and why each: a value that does not begin with a hyphen (a bare
# word reads as a positional argument, which is the prose inference the fence forbids), and a value
# carrying whitespace, a pipe or a backtick (it is interpolated into a markdown table row and a code
# span, and each of those three ends one of them early).
#
# NOTHING VERIFIES THE GESTURE. No script in this kit sees an invocation, so this key buys a token an
# agent can test for and never an authorization; the authorization stays the pushed build folder.
AUTH_PARAM=""

# UNITS_REGION_CUTOFF - the day THIS repo's build READMEs started carrying the generated
# `gen:build-units` region, as YYYY-MM-DD. It decides what a pinned BASE whose README carries NO such
# region means to the scope-integrity check in `check_authorization`.
#
# BLANK (the default, and what this example ships) is the OFF SWITCH: a BASE with no region keeps the
# older presence-based opt-in, so the check simply does not apply and nothing refuses. That is the
# right setting until you have rendered the region across your build folders - see the `--write` mode
# of the memory-tree kit's `gen_build_index.py`.
#
# Set it once the render has landed, to the day it landed. From that date onward a BASE with no region
# is a REFUSAL rather than a pass, because an absent region makes the subset comparison vacuously true
# - the scope would be "compared" against the empty set and every run would clear it. Leaving this
# blank forever is a supported choice; it means you have declined the check, not that you have passed
# it. Moving it later re-opens the vacuity for every BASE in between; moving it earlier refuses runs

# whose BASE predates the region and can never carry it.
UNITS_REGION_CUTOFF=""

# TOOL-aGradedMandate-4 - the date from which a CLOSED unit whose spec grades THIN blocks
# `build-complete`. THIN is the kit's own predicate: an empty section 2 Scope, section 6
# Acceptance criteria or section 7 Gates. Graded on the spec's FILENAME date, the same idiom
# UNITS_REGION_CUTOFF uses and for the same reason - two of 307 tracked CLOSED specs grade THIN
# today, both from a pre-kit July build, and a term that reds a landed spec no run may rewrite is
# unlandable. BLANK or absent turns the term OFF, and --close ANNOUNCES that it did rather than
# passing silently. Set to the day this landed.
SPEC_THIN_CUTOFF=""

PHASES_EXTRA=""
DOD_EXTRA=""

# The HALT VOCABULARY. A run that stops records WHY in a token,
# validated against the kit core set plus whatever a project appends here. The floor is
# shrink-only and REQUIRED: undeclared or malformed is a refusal, not a defaulted value,
# because a pin that quietly defaults is a pin nobody set.
HALT_CODES_EXTRA=""
HALT_FLOOR="7"

# The LANDER MARKER. A BARE NAME, resolved by the lander AND by `--landed` against
# `git rev-parse --git-common-dir` — never a tree-relative path, which names a different file in
# each half and cannot be written at all from a linked worktree, where `.git` is a FILE. When
# declared, the lander writes it on a successful push and `--landed` reads it — the only verb that
# runs after the push, so the only one that can observe it. What a COMPLIANT LANDER must write: a line naming the commit it pushed. Identity,
# not existence — a bare touched file is satisfied by any previous landing. Leave it EMPTY and
# the observation is simply not asked for; an adopter who has not adapted their lander is not
# wedged by a key they have never heard of.
LANDER_MARKER=""
# The two halves of the build method's condition 3, for a concurrently dispatched pass. A pass may not
# declare a write under SHARED_RECORDS; a GENERATED_INDEXES entry is refused only TOGETHER WITH its
# generator, because a generated index alone is what every ordinary pass touches. The run-state file
# is derived, never declared. Blank is the empty set; absent takes the kit default.
SHARED_RECORDS="memory/DECISIONS.md memory/backlog"
GENERATED_INDEXES="memory/LIVE.md:tools/memory-tree/gen_build_index.py memory/ledger:tools/memory-tree/gen_build_index.py"

# The date from which a LANDED record must name its anchor kind (`remote` or `local`). A record whose
# first commit predates it is read as `remote`, which is what every pre-existing record in fact is.
# Blank or absent grandfathers every record. Set it to the day you adopt the two-anchor landing.
LANDED_ANCHOR_CUTOFF=""

